<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Omsk</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Omsk is a city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a> and Om rivers. Founded as a fortress in 1716, it became the administrative centre of the Steppe region. In 1918–1919 it was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-kolchak">Admiral Kolchak</a>'s White government, and it is now a major industrial city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/omsk.jpg" alt="Omsk" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Крылов Иван, Siberiano, PetarM, LittleDrakon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Omsk_Collage_2016.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Omsk lies in the south of the West Siberian Plain, on the banks of the north-flowing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a> at its confluence with the Om River, which gives the city its name; in the dialect of the Baraba Tatars the hydronym means the quiet one. It stands about 450 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astana">Astana</a> and 2,230 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. Omsk is the junction of the northern and southern branches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-siberian-railway">Trans-Siberian Railway</a>, and its river port gives access to the waterways of the Irtysh and the Ob.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The fortress at Omsk arose from the need to secure the trade route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The Siberian governor, Prince Matvey Gagarin, obtained the tsar's consent in May 1714 to build fortresses along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a>. By the end of 1716 the first fortress, an earthen pentagon with stores, barracks and officers' houses, stood at the mouth of the Om. Omsk was granted town status in 1782, and in 1822 it became the administrative capital of Western Siberia. As the frontier receded the town became a major centre of Siberian exile; Fyodor Dostoyevsky served his sentence in an Omsk prison from 1850 to 1854. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-siberian-railway">Trans-Siberian Railway</a> of the 1890s made it a logistic hub.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> the Provisional All-Russian Government was established in Omsk in 1918 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-kolchak">Admiral Kolchak</a>, and the city was proclaimed capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, its central bank keeping the former empire's gold reserves. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> forced Kolchak to retreat east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irkutsk">Irkutsk</a>, and Bolshevik forces entered Omsk in 1919. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> gave the city new life, because industry was evacuated there from the front. In the 1950s the development of Siberian oil led to a refinery complex, now the largest in Russia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Omsk is the administrative centre and largest city of Omsk Oblast and has more than a million inhabitants. It is the third largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novosibirsk">Novosibirsk</a> and Krasnoyarsk. It is the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Omsk and Tara.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Omsk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
